  "query_preview": "..The large scale pattern has a storm system moving \nacross the northern tier of states and high pressure aloft centered \nover the northern Gulf and lower Mississippi Valley into Saturday. \nBy Sunday, a trough starts to dig south along the Pacific Coast as \nthe center of the high shifts westward into northeast Mexico and \nHurricane Polo moves northward along Mexico to west of the Baja tip. \nOn Monday into early Tuesday, an area of low pressure forms over \ncentral California within the deepening trough over California, as \nPolo starts to make an eastward curve into Gulf of California south \nof the Baja Spur. By Tuesday into early Wednesday, the upper low \nmoves from west to east across central Arizona dragging a frontal \nboundary/trough axis across the state, with the remnants of Polo \ntracking northeast across central Sonora and northern Chihuahua.   \n\nDrier air will continue to move into Arizona from the west through \nSaturday, as the storm system pushes east across the northern tier \nof states. There will be a slight chance of showers/thunderstorms \ninto the early evening hours tonight in northern Graham and Greenlee \ncounties and along the New Mexico state line, with dry conditions \nelsewhere. PWATs continue to lower on Saturday down into the 0.50 to \n0.75 inch range, with dry conditions areawide. \n\nBy Sunday, PWATS start to increase as the trough digs to our west \nand we start to tap moisture to our south. PWATs come up into the \n1.0 to 1.2 inch range by late in the day. There will be a slight \nchance of showers and thunderstorms Sunday afternoon and Sunday \nnight.  \n\nMonday and Monday night PWATs jump up into the 1.2 to 1.6 inch range \nas the trough deepens to our west and Hurricane Polo curves east \nacross the southern Baja Peninsula, the Gulf of California and then \nmakes landfall between Los Mochis and Guaymas. The flow across \nsoutheast Arizona at this time will be southerly tapping this deep \nmoisture, as the upper low and associated trough begin to push in \nfrom the west. Precipitation chances rise significantly across the \nforecast area during the day Monday into the 50-60 percent range \nacross much of the area, with near 80 percent POPs in the White \nMountains. The highest POPs areawide occur Monday night, with values \nin the 65-90 percent range.  \n\nTuesday into early Wednesday, the upper low and trough axis will \nshift eastward across the state and precipitation chances will \ngradually wind down from west to east. Across western Pima County \nthe precipitation chances are expected to end during the afternoon \nhours Tuesday and then late Tuesday night into the early morning \nhours Wednesday across eastern portions of the forecast area.  \n\nBehind the upper low and trough Wednesday, there will be a slight \nchance of showers and thunderstorms along the New Mexico state line, \nwith the rest of the forecast area remaining dry. High pressure \naloft then builds over the eastern Pacific Ocean and southeast \nArizona dries out significantly under westerly flow Thursday into \nthe following weekend. \n\nGiven the deep moisture across southeast Arizona Monday through \nTuesday and the strong dynamics associated with the upper low and \nfrontal boundary/trough axis there will likely be isolated severe \nthunderstorms over this time frame, along with the potential for \nflash flooding with any of the stronger thunderstorms. In fact, WPC \nhas already highlighted the eastern two-thirds of southeast Arizona \n(roughly Sells eastward) in a slight risk of excessive rainfall for \nMonday.  \n\nOtherwise, high temperatures will be near to slightly above normal \nover the weekend, lowering to 4-8 degrees below normal for most of \nnext week.",
